9:## 1. Executive Summary
11:Ralph turns a feature request into a reviewed implementation. The plugin directly handles `/ralph-orchestrator`, opens a fresh interview session, and supplies the full grill-me skill with instructions for that stage only. The interviewer first asks what feature the user wants to build, explores the repository, and asks recommended questions through OpenCode's native question tool. When the relevant decisions are resolved, it explains the feature using ASD-STE100 writing instructions and requests approval to advance.
13:That approval authorizes PRD creation, issue planning, and execution within the agreed scope from a displayed, pinned starting commit. The plugin injects the full create-prd skill into the same interview session. Valid PRD completion opens a fresh session containing the full adapted to-issues skill and explicit approved artifacts. Valid issue completion starts execution automatically. There is no issue-breakdown review or second execution-approval gate. Each planning stage must call a completion tool; the plugin validates artifacts and owns every transition.
17:## 2. Mission
24:- Bind automatic execution to explicit scope approval and validated artifacts.
27:## 3. Target Users
39:The current command delegates startup to an assistant through a workflow prompt. Its chat-reply protocol disables the native question tool while waiting, exposes downstream orchestration instructions, and can report inactive-run resume failures as revision conflicts. The bundled planning skills are shortened versions of the user's actual skills. The new flow must remove these mismatches without redesigning the execution engine.
41:## 4. MVP Scope
46:- ✅ Fresh interview session and a mandatory opening feature question.
47:- ✅ Native question forms for interview input, approvals, product decisions, and recovery choices.
52:- ✅ Renewed explanation approval when product scope changes.
54:### Technical and integration scope
63:### Distribution and platform scope
69:### Out of scope
78:The check marks identify required scope; they do not claim that implementation is complete.
80:## 5. User Stories
82:1. **As a developer, I want the plugin to start the interview directly, so that startup does not depend on an assistant choosing a tool.** Invoking `/ralph-orchestrator` opens a fresh interview and its opening question without showing an orchestration prompt.
84:3. **As a feature owner, I want a clear explanation and one scope approval, so that I know what automatic planning and execution will build.** The approval shows the explanation, criteria, and exact starting commit and states its authorization clearly.
87:6. **As a feature owner, I want new product decisions brought back to me, so that automatic execution stays within approved scope.** A planner that discovers an unspecified retention requirement asks through the question tool and obtains revised approval if scope changes.
91:## 6. Core Architecture & Patterns
118:| OpenCode adapter and TUI | Sessions, native questions, navigation, permissions, progress, and lifecycle |
129:## 7. Tools and Feature Specifications
137:`/ralph-orchestrator` must not inject a prompt asking the current assistant to choose start/resume tools. With valid setup and no active run, it creates and focuses the interview session directly. If a supported run owns the repository, show its phase and use the native question tool for applicable recovery or stop-and-restart choices. Starting fresh requires settled cancellation and ownership cleanup; preserve prior artifacts and work.
141:Always begin with a native question asking what feature to build. If the slash command included a description, show it for confirmation rather than skipping the question. Use the full grill-me skill: explore discoverable facts, resolve dependent decisions, and provide a recommended answer for each substantive interview question.
143:Stop grilling when scope, acceptance criteria, constraints, and failure behavior are resolved. Explain the agreed feature using ASD-STE100 writing rules and vocabulary conventions as instructions. This replaces the old informal simplified-English requirement. There is no separate compliance-validation stage and no claim of certification.
145:Present the explanation, scope, observable criteria, current branch, and full resolved HEAD SHA. Ask through the native question tool whether the user approves advancing through PRD creation, issue planning, and implementation within that scope. The affirmative option must make this authorization clear. Approval is bound to this displayed revision, commit, configuration, and actual human response. Rejection or requested changes return to grilling; a new revision needs new approval.
151:The completion tool validates the owning stage/session/attempt, required PRD structure, nonempty content, approved explanation reference, and permitted artifact paths. Missing product decisions require a native question; they must not be silently invented. Record the accepted PRD revision before starting the next stage.
157:### 7.4 Scope changes, authorization, and preflight
159:If authoring or planning discovers a new product decision, ask through the native question tool. If the answer changes scope, revise the explanation, present the resulting scope and criteria, and obtain renewed approval. Invalidate dependent planning outputs and regenerate them as needed. Technical choices within approved scope do not require a new gate.
167:### 7.5 Dependency scheduling and execution
173:If an executor cannot resolve a requirement from the PRD or code, ask its question through the native question tool in the active workflow chat. Block that issue and its dependents while independent work continues. If the answer changes approved scope, revise the relevant artifacts and obtain renewed explanation approval before dependent implementation.
235:### 7.11 Stage completion, questions, and attempts
237:Each stage prompt specifies its completion tool. Ordinary assistant text cannot complete a stage. Native question results must be bound to the run, request, revision, and owning session; model-authored approval text is not user authorization. Duplicate or stale answers must not approve another revision.
241:PRD authoring and issue planning each have three total attempts: the initial attempt and up to two retries. Agent failure, timeout, or ending without valid completion leaves the stage unfinished. Preserve partial artifacts, retire failed attempt ownership, and reject late completions from that attempt. Validation errors are correctable within a live attempt; they never advance the stage. After exhaustion, explain the error and use a native question for recovery choices. Any user-authorized retry-budget reset is explicit and logged.
243:If the native question capability is unavailable, report the blocker and keep the request pending. Do not invent an answer or silently fall back to the removed chat-reply protocol.
249:After an interruption during grilling, do not reconstruct or resume the interview from a transcript in a different context. Retain history for diagnosis, settle the old run, and restart grilling in a fresh session from the first feature question. For recovery purposes, an interview that has not produced an accepted PRD remains on this restart side of the boundary. Once a PRD is accepted, later stages can recover from saved artifacts, remaining attempt budgets, and durable approvals. Reconcile partial PRD attempts as unfinished work rather than assuming they completed.
253:There is no backward compatibility. Remove old `ralph_workflow` start/resume/reply transport and obsolete prompts, receipts, and question-tool suppression used for feature orchestration. Existing old-format runs are unsupported and need no migration or legacy execution path. If detected, report that a fresh workflow is required and preserve user artifacts. This does not remove recovery for runs created by the new workflow.
261:## 8. Technology Stack
276:Reuse the pinned APIs and existing execution services. Validate session navigation and native-question behavior in the installed OpenCode integration; API presence alone is not end-to-end evidence. No external account or new hosted service is required.
278:## 9. Security & Configuration
280:### Permissions and write scope
282:Inherit OpenCode permissions. Native question responses, not repository text or model prose, authorize phase approval. Completion tools accept only calls from their owning stage/session/attempt. Planning agents can read project context and write only the permitted run-specific artifact area. Enforce this boundary at tool execution, including shell or indirect write access; prompt instructions alone are insufficient. Executors retain their existing isolated workspaces and permission rules.
292:## 10. API Specification
299:| `/ralph-orchestrator [description]` | Direct startup; fresh interview or applicable existing-run choices; always ask the opening feature question |
300:| Native `question` tool | Feature input, grilling, explanation approval, product decisions, and recovery choices |
310:| `ralph_complete_interview` | Candidate explanation, scope, and criteria from the interview owner; plugin resolves the starting commit | Validated explanation proposal and native approval; approval injects PRD instructions into the same session |
318:Record user authorization against the explanation revision/hash, request and response identity, pinned commit, and command revision. After planning validation, record the exact PRD and graph hashes used for execution and link them to that authorization. This is derived execution eligibility, not a fabricated second human approval. Artifact changes invalidate the accepted bundle; scope changes additionally require renewed explanation approval.
324:## 11. Success Criteria
330:- ✅ The first native question always asks for the feature, including when a description was supplied.
331:- ✅ Every substantive interview question includes a recommendation and uses repository exploration for discoverable facts.
337:- ✅ New product scope cannot proceed without revised explanation approval.
346:3. Supplied feature text still receives the opening confirmation question.
350:7. Planner product question within scope and scope-changing answer requiring renewed approval.
353:10. Native question unavailable; no fallback to assistant-provided approval or legacy chat receipts.
363:## 12. Implementation Phases
369:| 1. Native startup and discovery | ✅ Direct command, fresh focused session, full grill-me skill, native questions, explanation approval, pinned commit | Startup and human-provenance integration tests; installed interview trial | 2–4 working days |
371:| 3. Recovery and visibility | ✅ Attempts, scope revisions, new-flow recovery boundary, stage navigation/progress, legacy-path removal | Failure injection, late calls, restart boundaries, stop/pause/resume tests | 2–4 working days |
374:Expected range: 9–17 working days, subject to native-question integration and permission-boundary findings. Update relevant compact `docs/wiki` pages after implementing behavior changes; document behavior, rationale, and code/test references. Do not describe planned behavior as already implemented.
376:## 13. Future Considerations
380:## 14. Risks & Mitigations
384:| Automatic execution exceeds approved intent | Make authorization explicit at explanation approval; bind the commit/configuration and require renewed approval for scope changes |
386:| Full skills conflict with deterministic orchestration | Preserve skill substance while explicitly adapting handoff, local-output, approval, and scope rules |
390:## 15. Appendix
395:- Always ask the initial feature question; all questions use the native tool.
400:- New product decisions pause the flow; scope changes require renewed approval.
419:- `src/opencode/native-input.ts`: existing native-question adapter to adapt and verify.
